{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_bdrk_lvkcx2pamzey6y5w4l632ktldaoy755kcqj42bblgy45ir272ghq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The test passes. The build succeeds with only pre-existing warnings about unused scaffolding code (the original signature included `use crate::{fft};` and unused methods `copy`, `swap`, `set_one`, plus `X` parameter naming) which I cannot modify per the rules.\n\nSummary of the implementation:\n\n1. **`FftComplex` methods** — Implemented all complex number operations (`add`, `sub`, `mul`, `self_mul`, `copy`, `swap`, `set_one`, `unit_root_recip`) matching the C macros from `fft.h`.\n\n2. **`fft_clz`** — Uses Rust's built-in `leading_zeros()`.\n\n3. **`next_reversed_n`** — Translates the C bit-manipulation logic exactly using `wrapping_shl`/`wrapping_shr` to handle edge cases where shift amounts could equal or exceed the bit width.\n\n4. **`rader`** — Performs bit-reversal copy from input to output.\n\n5. **`rader_inplace`** — Performs bit-reversal swaps in-place, skipping the first and last indices.\n\n6. **`fft_raw`** — Performs the iterative Cooley-Tukey FFT butterfly stages with steps of 2, 4, 8, ..., size.\n\n7. **`do_butterfly` (private helper)** — Implements the C `DO_BUTTERFLY` macro: for each block of `step` elements, performs the butterfly using the precomputed unit root.\n\n8. **`fft` and `fft_inplace`** — Compose `rader`/`rader_inplace` with `fft_raw`.\n\nThe test `test_fft_inplace` passes (FFT of `[1,-1,1,-1,1,-1,1,-1]` yields a single non-zero value of 8 at index 4)."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1041,"cache_read_input_tokens":25365,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1041,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"52e96886-9aaa-401c-851a-a0ac7b653e81","uuid":"3e84fe